{0 Timers, time, and randomness}

Use {!Workero.Timer} to wait in a handler, {!Workero.Date} to read the wall
clock, and {!Workero.Crypto} to generate random values. This handler waits
before returning a random UUID.

{@ocaml[
let handler _req _env _ctx =
  let open Workero in
  Promise.await (Timer.sleep 100);
  Response.text (Crypto.random_uuid () ^ "\n")

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

Run [npx wrangler dev] in one terminal and [curl http://localhost:8787/] in a
second terminal. The response is a version 4 UUID.

{1 Timers}

{!Workero.Timer.sleep}, {!Workero.Timer.set_timeout}, and
{!Workero.Timer.set_interval} take delays in milliseconds. Use
{!Workero.Timer.clear_timeout} or {!Workero.Timer.clear_interval} to stop a
timer. Call timers from a handler or {!Workero.Context.wait_until}, not at
startup. A timer callback cannot call {!Workero.Promise.await}; use
{!Workero.Timer.sleep} inside a handler when you need to wait.

{1 Time}

{!Workero.Date.now} is [Date.now()]. {!Workero.Date.to_iso_string} is
[new Date(t).toISOString()]. Times are milliseconds since the Unix epoch, as a
[float]. In Workers the clock moves only after I/O. It does not measure CPU
time.

{1 Randomness}

{!Workero.Crypto.random_uuid} returns a random version 4 UUID.
{!Workero.Crypto.random_bytes} returns cryptographically random bytes. It
accepts at most 65536 bytes per call and raises [Invalid_argument] for a
negative size or a larger request.

{1 Subtle crypto}

{!Workero.Crypto} also wraps a first subset of [crypto.subtle]. Data goes in and
out as OCaml strings that hold raw bytes. The functions wait for the runtime, so
call them inside a handler.

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let sign_and_check secret body =
  let key = Crypto.Key.import_hmac Crypto.Sha256 secret in
  let signature = Crypto.hmac_sign key body in
  Crypto.hmac_verify key ~signature body

let handler _req _env _ctx =
  Response.text (string_of_bool (sign_and_check "secret" "body"))

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

[sign_and_check] gives [true] for any non-empty [secret]. This function
encrypts and decrypts with AES-GCM. [raw_key] has 16, 24 or 32 bytes. It gives
[Some plain]:

{@ocaml[
open Workero

let seal_and_open raw_key plain =
  let key = Crypto.Key.import_aes_gcm raw_key in
  let iv = Crypto.random_bytes 12 in
  let sealed = Crypto.aes_gcm_encrypt key ~iv plain in
  Crypto.aes_gcm_decrypt key ~iv sealed

let handler _req _env _ctx =
  match seal_and_open (String.make 16 'k') "hello" with
  | Some plain -> Response.text plain
  | None -> Response.text ~status:`Internal_Server_Error "decryption failed"

let () = Workero.serve handler
]}

- {!Workero.Crypto.digest}: SHA-1, SHA-256, SHA-384 and SHA-512, chosen by
  {!Workero.Crypto.hash}.
- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_hmac}, {!Workero.Crypto.hmac_sign} and
  {!Workero.Crypto.hmac_verify}.
- {!Workero.Crypto.Key.import_aes_gcm}, {!Workero.Crypto.aes_gcm_encrypt} and
  {!Workero.Crypto.aes_gcm_decrypt}. The cipher text ends with the 16-byte tag.
  Never use one [iv] twice with one key. Decryption gives [None] when
  authentication fails.
- {!Workero.Crypto.pbkdf2} and {!Workero.Crypto.hkdf} derive bytes.

A {!Workero.Crypto.Key.t} is a non-extractable [CryptoKey]. Its type names the algorithm, so
the compiler rejects an HMAC key in an AES-GCM function. Bad arguments that the
library can check raise [Invalid_argument]. A rejection from the runtime raises
{!Workero.Js_error}. Deployed Workers allow at most 100000 PBKDF2
iterations.

For logging, use {{!page-logging}Console or standard output}. For other
globals, see {{!page-interop}JavaScript interop} and
{{:https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/web-standards/}supported web standards}.
